Usage: marbor runtime [action] [args] [flags]

Actions:
  start <node>    start the node's inference runtime process
  stop <node>     stop the node's inference runtime process
  restart <node>  restart the node's inference runtime process
  logs <node>     fetch recent log lines from the node's runtime process
  drain <node>    mark the node draining (stop routing new requests to it)
  undrain <node>  reverse "runtime drain"
  health <node>   run an on-demand active liveness probe on the node

Flags:
  --server string    Admin API base URL (default "http://localhost:8080", env MARBOR_SERVER)
  --json             output machine-readable JSON instead of a human table
  --username string  admin username, used to log in (env MARBOR_USERNAME); must be given together with --password
  --password string  admin password, used to log in (env MARBOR_PASSWORD); must be given together with --username

"start|stop|restart" requires the target node to have an operator-accepted control driver (see "node control accept") - a node with none configured returns an error rather than guessing one.

"stop|restart" on the head, a worker, or an unresolved member of a multi-host replica is rejected unless --acknowledge-replica is passed: stopping any member breaks the replica, and marbor does not restart the other members. "drain" on a replica member is allowed and prints what it does: draining a worker has no routing effect, draining the head drains the replica.

"drain" and "restart" first check whether the node is the only server of any model it holds. If it is, they ask for confirmation on a terminal, or exit 1 without a terminal unless --force is passed. Models that only an Ollama node could load on demand, and places where other nodes cannot be verified, are printed but never block. --dry-run prints the check and changes nothing. The check covers drains and restarts made through marbor only.

The interactive confirm is scoped to the list it printed: marbor sends that list with the request, and the server changes nothing, prints the new list and exits 1 if another model has lost its last server since. --force skips the prompt and does not check the list. A script can get the same scoping from the Admin API: GET /admin/nodes/{name}/sole-replica, then POST the drain or restart with force=true and one acknowledge_sole=<model> per model listed, and on a 409 review the new list and repeat. Models match by name, so one entry covers that model on every runtime. The list is sent in the URL, so a very long one can hit a proxy's request-line limit: the CLI refuses above 6000 encoded bytes and --force is the way past it.

"logs" is a point-in-time snapshot, not a live tail. A node whose control driver has no real log source (e.g. a bare PID-file process with no supervisor) returns a clear "not supported" error.

requires credentials: run "marbor login" once (recommended), or pass --username+--password (or MARBOR_USERNAME+MARBOR_PASSWORD).
